1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,600
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

2
00:00:09,601 --> 00:00:13,500
Today, history unfolds before our eyes.

3
00:00:13,501 --> 00:00:18,680
Everyone can be an eyewitness, can feel the impact of events as they happen.

4
00:00:18,681 --> 00:00:22,500
It's all done through the marvel of television.

5
00:00:22,501 --> 00:00:25,400
Television gives us much more than a front row seat.

6
00:00:25,401 --> 00:00:28,400
It brings amazing events right into our living rooms.

7
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:30,400
Challenger, go with throttle up.

8
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,400
With a push of a button, a window opens on the world.

9
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,400
Through it, we can be deeply moved.

10
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,400
We're informed about the world around us.

11
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:55,400
And we can be inspired to greatness.

12
00:00:55,401 --> 00:00:57,400
I have a dream.

13
00:00:57,401 --> 00:01:02,400
But television also entertains us, helps us to suspend reality.

14
00:01:02,401 --> 00:01:04,400
Bob, now give me my pants.

15
00:01:04,401 --> 00:01:07,400
I'll give them them.

16
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:12,400
Pull your head back in or I'll hand out baseballs to the audience.

17
00:01:12,401 --> 00:01:19,400
Today, television is commonplace, but not so long ago, it was the stuff of science fiction.

18
00:01:19,401 --> 00:01:25,460
television's journey from the laboratory to the living room was filled with incredible discoveries

19
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:34,380
near misses and extraordinary ambition behind the fascinating technology men of genius explored a

20
00:01:34,381 --> 00:01:42,440
new uncharted electronic frontier and took us all on a journey into the future probably nothing

21
00:01:42,441 --> 00:01:47,640
since the printing press has been as much of an influence

22
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:52,820
on the culture and the mindset of the peoples of the world.

23
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:58,520
Television, window to the world.

24
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:26,440
The story of television begins in an unlikely way.

25
00:02:31,620 --> 00:02:33,620
The year was 1922.

26
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,620
Ford's Model T sold faster than the factory could turn them out.

27
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:41,600
Cities were buzzing with the newfound power of electricity.

28
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:48,600
early technology was transforming the landscape electricity was a revolution in science that had

29
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:56,520
begun over 100 years before in the early 1800s europeans like george ohm and heinrich hertz

30
00:02:56,521 --> 00:03:03,080
formerly trained scientists slowly uncovered one of nature's most complex mysteries electricity

31
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:10,120
in america men like alexander graham bell and thomas edison turned electrical theory

32
00:03:10,121 --> 00:03:12,580
into mechanical reality.

33
00:03:12,581 --> 00:03:15,900
Inventions like the phonograph and the motion picture camera

34
00:03:15,901 --> 00:03:19,500
were the result of a little science and a lot of ingenuity.

35
00:03:19,501 --> 00:03:21,700
Men without formal training were making

36
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:24,040
extraordinary breakthroughs.

37
00:03:24,041 --> 00:03:27,640
One such inventor was about to make a quantum leap in science.

38
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,740
He was Guillermo Marconi.

39
00:03:29,741 --> 00:03:32,880
And in 1896, when he sent an electric impulse

40
00:03:32,881 --> 00:03:35,920
through thin air, wireless was born.

41
00:03:35,921 --> 00:03:37,880
Before Marconi, telegraph messages

42
00:03:37,881 --> 00:03:41,320
could only be sent to one location and only by wire.

43
00:03:41,321 --> 00:03:43,640
Now, completely wireless messages

44
00:03:43,641 --> 00:03:46,420
could be sent in all directions at once.

45
00:03:46,421 --> 00:03:49,140
The next challenge was to try and send a voice

46
00:03:49,141 --> 00:03:51,080
over the airwaves.

47
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:01,280
In 1908, that's exactly what inventor Lee DeForest did.

48
00:04:01,281 --> 00:04:04,600
He broadcast the first human voice from the Eiffel Tower.

49
00:04:04,601 --> 00:04:09,060
He had invented a delicate and complex electronic device, the Audion tube.

50
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:13,760
By capturing electrons, it could transmit voice patterns over the airwaves.

51
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:19,160
It was into this exciting world of invention that a 14-year-old boy came along

52
00:04:19,161 --> 00:04:21,940
who would become critical to the development of television.

53
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:28,760
On a remote farm in Beaver, Utah, a Mormon schoolboy dreamed of taking DeForest's work a step further.

54
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:33,700
Philo T. Farnsworth had no formal training in the new field of electronics,

55
00:04:33,701 --> 00:04:38,700
But as a teenager, he was destined to change the 20th century.

56
00:04:38,701 --> 00:04:43,700
Young Farnsworth was determined to come up with an electronic tube of his own,

57
00:04:43,701 --> 00:04:46,700
one that would carry both sound and pictures.

58
00:04:46,701 --> 00:04:50,700
Reading scientific journals by candlelight, he worked late into the night

59
00:04:50,701 --> 00:04:54,700
in an attempt to decode the complex phenomenon of particle physics.

60
00:04:54,701 --> 00:04:58,700
At 14, the young genius was ready to share his idea.

61
00:04:58,701 --> 00:05:02,700
While the other kids brought cut and paste objects to show and tell,

62
00:05:02,701 --> 00:05:05,200
Farnsworth brought something else.

63
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:08,200
He put some of the equations for it on the blackboard,

64
00:05:08,201 --> 00:05:11,200
and his professor said, his science professor said,

65
00:05:11,201 --> 00:05:12,200
what's that?

66
00:05:12,201 --> 00:05:15,200
And he said, well, it's a means of projecting television

67
00:05:15,201 --> 00:05:17,200
images over the radio.

68
00:05:17,201 --> 00:05:21,200
The theory was so complex that his teacher sent Farnsworth

69
00:05:21,201 --> 00:05:25,200
to Brigham Young University to see if the boy was crazy or

70
00:05:25,201 --> 00:05:26,200
a genius.

71
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:29,200
At the university, the scientists were astonished.

72
00:05:29,201 --> 00:05:29,933
astonished.

73
00:05:29,934 --> 00:05:34,800
Basically the idea was that an image would be read by a lens and captured

74
00:05:34,801 --> 00:05:35,510
on a plate.

75
00:05:35,511 --> 00:05:40,480
The image on the plate would be scanned by a beam of lightning fast electrons

76
00:05:40,481 --> 00:05:43,030
emanating from a cathode ray tube.

77
00:05:43,031 --> 00:05:47,080
Electrons would bounce back off the plate representing

78
00:05:47,081 --> 00:05:49,184
light and dark areas of the image.

79
00:05:49,185 --> 00:05:52,960
The electrons in the tube would be converted into an electric

80
00:05:52,961 --> 00:05:57,660
impulse, which could then be sent to a transmitter and out over the airways.

81
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:03,604
This image would, in turn, be carried to a receiver.

82
00:06:03,605 --> 00:06:06,500
The image, or signal, would be amplified

83
00:06:06,501 --> 00:06:11,680
and beamed onto the inside of the chemically treated tube, in much the same way it had

84
00:06:11,681 --> 00:06:15,281
been captured. This was the televised image.

85
00:06:15,282 --> 00:06:18,800
But television would take a long, difficult

86
00:06:18,801 --> 00:06:21,950
journey from the drawing board to the living room.

87
00:06:21,951 --> 00:06:25,100
Unfortunately, Philo Farnsworth would have to wait

88
00:06:25,101 --> 00:06:26,383
to prove his theory.

89
00:06:26,384 --> 00:06:31,260
After a year in college, his father's death forced him to drop out of school

90
00:06:31,261 --> 00:06:32,000
and seek work.

91
00:06:32,001 --> 00:06:35,860
It looked as though the young inventor would never get the opportunity to

92
00:06:35,861 --> 00:06:38,924
actually create his television.

93
00:06:38,925 --> 00:06:45,940
Across the country, a different kind of genius was about to embark on a

94
00:06:45,941 --> 00:06:51,820
towards his career in television. David Sarnoff would do more than anyone else to

95
00:06:51,821 --> 00:06:56,020
shape the future of television. And considering where he started, it's

96
00:06:56,021 --> 00:07:01,600
remarkable that he ever got the chance. At 14, the young Russian immigrant wanted

97
00:07:01,601 --> 00:07:06,700
to escape the grinding poverty of New York's teeming Lower East Side. Forced

98
00:07:06,701 --> 00:07:11,680
to drop out of school and look for a job, a lucky twist of fate would alter Sarnoff's

99
00:07:11,681 --> 00:07:16,600
future a wrong turn on the way to an interview landed him in the offices of

100
00:07:16,601 --> 00:07:22,680
American Marconi the inventor of the wireless Marconi needed a messenger and

101
00:07:22,681 --> 00:07:27,600
Sarnoff needed a job but he got more than expected from the chance encounter

102
00:07:27,601 --> 00:07:31,720
and he said from the first day there was something almost hypnotic about wireless

103
00:07:31,721 --> 00:07:37,660
to him his Marconi was his idol he dressed the way Marconi dress use the

104
00:07:37,661 --> 00:07:42,600
that Marconi used, gloves, suits, things like that.

105
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:45,300
He idolized it.

106
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,240
Sarnoff had an intuitive feeling

107
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,280
for the new world of electronics.

108
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:53,580
He wasn't an inventor, but he possessed his own unique gifts.

109
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:56,720
And one of these gifts was a pension for promotion.

110
00:07:56,820 --> 00:08:00,680
According to legend, on the night of April 15, 1912,

111
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:04,160
Sarnoff was manning the Marconi Relay Station in New York

112
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:06,360
when he picked up an urgent message.

113
00:08:06,361 --> 00:08:08,540
A boat was sinking in the Atlantic.

114
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,080
It was the Titanic.

115
00:08:12,780 --> 00:08:15,560
Sarnoff worked the wireless key for three straight nights,

116
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:17,200
alerting other ships in the area.

117
00:08:17,940 --> 00:08:21,880
Years later, it was established that the story may have been partly myth.

118
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:26,180
Nonetheless, Sarnoff repeated the story so often it gained currency

119
00:08:26,181 --> 00:08:28,760
and gained Sarnoff a reputation.

120
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:32,000
As Sarnoff worked his way up the ranks at Marconi,

121
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:35,180
more and more people began using the airwaves to send messages.

122
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:40,820
The problem was that the space for sending signals was limited, which caused interference.

123
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:45,320
The airwaves needed to be organized or chaos would result.

124
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:48,800
So the government stepped in to solve the problem.

125
00:08:50,040 --> 00:08:51,858
Secretary of the Navy Franklin D.

126
00:08:51,859 --> 00:08:56,760
Roosevelt gathered all the companies with a stake in this new phenomenon called wireless.

127
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,140
Together they formed a trust called RCA.

128
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:06,860
The idea was to put all of Broadcasting's eggs in one basket and then watch that basket.

129
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:14,860
RCA's first step was to buy out Marconi's American operation, keeping it under American control.

130
00:09:15,860 --> 00:09:18,440
RCA got a better deal than they realized.

131
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:23,640
Marconi Company came with the self-assured, determined David Sarnoff.

132
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:27,080
He had vast self-confidence.

133
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:30,060
He had the biggest ego of any man I've ever seen.

134
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:34,020
It's hard to believe, but he once told me, he said, I've never known fear.

135
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:42,720
Now, that to me is a remarkable statement, but he said it as though he really believed it.

136
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:49,940
Sarnoff would one day become the father of television, but first he would transform radio.

137
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,420
At 22, Sarnoff outlined his vision for radio in a brief document known as the Radio Box Memo.

138
00:09:58,140 --> 00:10:03,340
Sarnoff envisioned a device in the home where people could receive music, news, and sports.

139
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:10,680
The memo outlined an organized system of regular programming, five years before it happened.

140
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:14,360
But Sarnoff's idea received a cool reception.

141
00:10:15,140 --> 00:10:17,440
The technical challenges hadn't yet been overcome,

142
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:23,620
and even though it was possible to broadcast voice and music, why would anyone want to?

143
00:10:23,621 --> 00:10:31,780
While Sarnoff struggled to gain acceptance, Philo Farnsworth was still looking for a break

144
00:10:31,781 --> 00:10:33,264
in Salt Lake City.

145
00:10:33,265 --> 00:10:39,120
As with all great inventors, luck was about to play a fateful role. One

146
00:10:39,121 --> 00:10:42,938
of his bosses introduced Farnsworth to wealthy friends.

147
00:10:42,939 --> 00:10:45,160
Farnsworth's idea for television

148
00:10:45,161 --> 00:10:49,236
captivated them, and they agreed to finance his work.

149
00:10:49,237 --> 00:10:51,620
Farnsworth moved to Los Angeles

150
00:10:51,621 --> 00:10:53,420
where he could find necessary materials.

151
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:57,700
The young inventor had to invent whole new tools just to proceed.

152
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:02,240
He mixed chemicals, even blew the glass for his experimental tubes,

153
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:04,340
all in utmost secrecy.

154
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:09,460
He was concerned someone might steal the idea and file for precious patents.

155
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:15,400
Farnsworth spent every waking moment trying to perfect his new invention.

156
00:11:15,401 --> 00:11:18,020
He confessed to his new bride, Penn,

157
00:11:18,021 --> 00:11:21,940
there's another woman in my life and her name is television.

158
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:26,400
Farnsworth was close to creating a working television,

159
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:27,840
but the clock was ticking.

160
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:31,180
He wasn't the only inventor trying to solve the mystery.

161
00:11:31,500 --> 00:11:35,780
He had competition, respected scientists who were close at his heels.

162
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:45,940
In 1920, while Farnsworth struggled with his idea for television,

163
00:11:45,941 --> 00:11:48,300
radio began finding its voice.

164
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:52,200
In Pittsburgh, a Westinghouse engineer named Frank Conrad

165
00:11:52,201 --> 00:11:56,200
began experimental radio broadcasts with voice and music.

166
00:11:56,720 --> 00:12:00,160
From the Westinghouse rooftop on Election Day, November 2nd,

167
00:12:00,420 --> 00:12:05,060
the first radio station in America, KDKA, went on the air.

168
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:09,040
We'd appreciate it if anyone hearing this broadcast would communicate with us,

169
00:12:09,420 --> 00:12:12,740
as we are very anxious to know how far the broadcast is reaching.

170
00:12:12,741 --> 00:12:18,640
The idea, a long time coming, caught on and broadcast fever had begun.

171
00:12:19,540 --> 00:12:21,980
At RCA, Sarnoff wasn't surprised.

172
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:26,220
He'd known it all along, and Westinghouse's success proved him right.

173
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:34,760
The demand for radios, transmitters, towers, and stations exploded, and so did the financial rewards.

174
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:40,360
RCA now looked to Sarnoff to transform their wireless business into radio.

175
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:43,980
Radio improved the value of the broadcasting.

176
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:49,260
Philo Farnsworth wanted to prove that images, too, could be transmitted.

177
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:52,300
The race for television was heating up.

178
00:13:01,180 --> 00:13:05,140
In the 1920s, America was dancing the Charleston.

179
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,380
Prohibition was casting its dry shadow,

180
00:13:08,381 --> 00:13:10,800
and radio was the toast of the town.

181
00:13:11,420 --> 00:13:15,720
Young David Sarnoff's brilliance was rewarded with a lucrative position at RCA.

182
00:13:16,700 --> 00:13:21,720
But soon he had begun casting his visionary eye across the technological landscape.

183
00:13:22,520 --> 00:13:24,880
It came to rest on television.

184
00:13:26,460 --> 00:13:28,480
And Farnsworth now had company.

185
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:31,680
Real scientists were getting into the race for television.

186
00:13:32,340 --> 00:13:36,100
One of them, a brilliant Russian immigrant named Vladimir Zworikin,

187
00:13:36,101 --> 00:13:38,860
had begun to figure out the puzzle of television

188
00:13:38,861 --> 00:13:41,440
at precisely the same time as Farnsworth.

189
00:13:42,620 --> 00:13:44,560
Remarkably, the two men's experiments

190
00:13:44,561 --> 00:13:46,640
were nearly identical in concept.

191
00:13:47,940 --> 00:13:50,460
Zwarikin was quickly making a name for himself

192
00:13:50,461 --> 00:13:52,520
in an unlikely way.

193
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:55,000
One of his experiments blew up,

194
00:13:55,540 --> 00:13:59,340
and that's how his bosses first took notice of him.

195
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,320
Zwarikin always liked to push the edges in his experiments,

196
00:14:03,321 --> 00:14:08,140
which is part of the reason why he hit on the invention of television.

197
00:14:08,840 --> 00:14:13,380
The brash young scientist tried to get Westinghouse to fully back his invention,

198
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:17,860
but the conservative company wasn't interested in Zwarikin's wild idea,

199
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:21,500
and television was in a highly experimental stage.

200
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:25,820
Scientists hadn't yet agreed on a standard system for the television.

201
00:14:26,580 --> 00:14:30,460
In theory, there were two possible ways to capture and reproduce an image.

202
00:14:30,461 --> 00:14:39,000
Both Zworikin and Farnsworth's electronic television relied on tiny charged electrons scanning and then amplifying the image.

203
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:44,780
The competitor was mechanical television, which relied on a rotating disc with small holes.

204
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:50,140
Light beamed through these rotating holes could also create television scan lines.

205
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:57,580
Mechanical television was less complex, but electronic had the potential to create a far better image.

206
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:04,700
Convinced electronic could work, Farnsworth moved to San Francisco,

207
00:15:04,980 --> 00:15:06,794
where the wealthy banker W.H.

208
00:15:06,795 --> 00:15:12,360
Crocker backed his plan to develop the potentially superior electronic television system.

209
00:15:13,300 --> 00:15:18,140
In 1925, after five years of hard work, Farnsworth was ready.

210
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:23,860
He set up a demonstration guaranteed to thrill the public and capture the world's attention.

211
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:30,140
Farnsworth invited the wildly popular Mary Pickford and Douglas Fairbanks up from Los Angeles

212
00:15:30,141 --> 00:15:33,080
to be part of a demonstration in San Francisco.

213
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,840
Unfortunately, things didn't go quite as planned.

214
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:39,320
Farnsworth had the demonstration all set up.

215
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:40,560
It worked perfectly in rehearsal,

216
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:43,760
and then when these super celebrities from Hollywood sat down,

217
00:15:44,100 --> 00:15:45,880
the whole thing didn't happen.

218
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:49,260
The screen went blank, and the celebrities went back,

219
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:51,440
convinced, like the rest of the nation,

220
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:52,840
that there was no future in television.

221
00:15:54,620 --> 00:15:57,060
Farnsworth needed to produce results quickly.

222
00:15:57,061 --> 00:16:00,340
His failed demonstration worried his backers.

223
00:16:00,820 --> 00:16:02,000
The pressure was growing.

224
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:05,360
Others were placing their bets on mechanical television.

225
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:10,560
Mechanical television's great proponent was Charles Francis Jenkins.

226
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:14,420
He was a highly respected inventor of the self-starting engine

227
00:16:14,421 --> 00:16:17,020
and the 35-millimeter motion picture projector.

228
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:20,840
Jenkins' fast work and flair for publicity

229
00:16:20,841 --> 00:16:23,780
put him out ahead of the pack very quickly.

230
00:16:23,781 --> 00:16:32,060
In a 1927 publicity coup, Jenkins unveiled the first long-distance television transmission in history.

231
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:36,360
Jenkins convinced Herbert Hoover, then Secretary of Commerce,

232
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:41,640
to take part in sending an intercity television image from Washington to New York.

233
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:50,420
After first burning his forehead on the hot lights, a crude 48-line image appeared on the screen.

234
00:16:50,421 --> 00:16:54,920
The future president spoke for two minutes, followed by his wife.

235
00:16:54,921 --> 00:16:59,420
She was concerned the device not be used to read minds.

236
00:16:59,421 --> 00:17:03,420
Mechanical television had struck the first blow.

237
00:17:03,421 --> 00:17:10,420
But scientists took Farnsworth's work in electronic television seriously.

238
00:17:10,421 --> 00:17:15,420
England was becoming the first battleground to decide the future format of television.

239
00:17:15,421 --> 00:17:19,420
England had its own inventor, James Baird,

240
00:17:19,421 --> 00:17:25,420
an eccentric Scotsman best known for his invention of the electric sock for soldiers in World War I.

241
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:31,040
As early as 1923, Baird had built a working mechanical television,

242
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:36,420
and he was about to convince the BBC to adopt mechanical as the standard for England.

243
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:43,520
But before they endorsed Baird's plan, the BBC wanted to see this electronic TV of Philo Farnsworth.

244
00:17:44,460 --> 00:17:47,800
Both men were invited to demonstrate their competing systems.

245
00:17:49,420 --> 00:17:55,680
When the momentous day came, Baird showed his television first to enthusiastic response.

246
00:17:56,700 --> 00:18:03,260
While Farnsworth began his demonstration in the next room, Baird was unprepared for what he saw next.

247
00:18:03,261 --> 00:18:08,540
And out of the corner of his eye, he caught the Farnsworth image on a very large screen.

248
00:18:09,400 --> 00:18:15,540
And he just stopped mid-sentence and wandered away from the people he was talking to back into the open door

249
00:18:15,541 --> 00:18:20,980
to get a clearer look at this incredibly superior image that Farnsworth had produced.

250
00:18:20,981 --> 00:18:25,220
And Baird knew at that moment that he would just be a footnote in television history.

251
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:30,840
The next day, British Parliament voted to license Farnsworth's system.

252
00:18:31,520 --> 00:18:33,080
Baird was devastated.

253
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:36,120
Television was still in its infancy.

254
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:38,180
An image had barely been transmitted.

255
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:41,860
But a few people were able to catch a glimpse of this new invention,

256
00:18:41,861 --> 00:18:46,860
including a young high school student who would make his own impact on television.

257
00:18:46,861 --> 00:18:54,860
1933, at the World's Fair in Chicago, with a group of us who saved our money and went up from Houston, Texas to Chicago,

258
00:18:54,861 --> 00:18:57,538
and they had an exhibit of television there.

259
00:18:57,539 --> 00:19:01,860
I was one of those who volunteered to get up and have my picture taken,

260
00:19:01,861 --> 00:19:07,860
and it was shown on the other side of the room with great amazement of everybody present.

261
00:19:11,860 --> 00:19:20,780
Philo Farnsworth was America's boy genius of television, but he had competition.

262
00:19:21,560 --> 00:19:25,740
The explosive and brilliant physicist Zworekin was every bit his equal.

263
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:32,980
At Westinghouse, Zworekin's ideas fell on deaf ears, but his luck was about to change.

264
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:37,240
Zworekin decided to pitch his idea to David Sarnoff at RCA,

265
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:41,480
a meeting that would change the course of broadcasting history.

266
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:46,860
Sarnoff had an intuitive understanding for the vast potential of television.

267
00:19:46,861 --> 00:19:53,860
In Zwartgen, he found a technological soulmate, someone capable of making the idea a working reality.

268
00:19:53,861 --> 00:19:56,860
Soon, Zwartgen began working for Sarnoff.

269
00:19:56,861 --> 00:20:00,860
Philo T. Farnsworth now had serious competition.

270
00:20:00,861 --> 00:20:03,860
Zwartgen now had the megalith RCA behind him.

271
00:20:03,861 --> 00:20:06,860
Both men claimed they had invented television.

272
00:20:06,861 --> 00:20:08,860
And both men were right.

273
00:20:08,861 --> 00:20:15,860
Since Farnsworth and Zwerakin had done their work concurrently, the patent courts would have to decide who had been first.

274
00:20:15,861 --> 00:20:19,860
The deciding factor was Farnsworth's high school science teacher.

275
00:20:19,861 --> 00:20:24,860
He had copied the drawings of the 14-year-old boy off a Utah blackboard.

276
00:20:24,861 --> 00:20:28,860
This was proof that Farnsworth's ideas had come first.

277
00:20:28,861 --> 00:20:31,860
Farnsworth got his patents.

278
00:20:31,861 --> 00:20:36,860
The small-town farm boy had taken on the giant corporation and won.

279
00:20:36,861 --> 00:20:41,360
RCA now needed Farnsworth's patents to further develop television.

280
00:20:41,361 --> 00:20:45,860
They tried to pressure Farnsworth to sell his new patents to RCA,

281
00:20:45,861 --> 00:20:48,860
but Farnsworth wanted continuous royalties.

282
00:20:48,861 --> 00:20:51,860
Sarnoff had never allowed such a thing.

283
00:20:51,861 --> 00:20:55,860
RCA had built their entire fortune on ownership of patents.

284
00:20:55,861 --> 00:21:00,360
Sarnoff did all he could to intimidate the 24-year-old inventor.

285
00:21:00,361 --> 00:21:05,860
General Sarnoff had a very dominant type of personality.

286
00:21:05,861 --> 00:21:08,700
I've seen people absolutely terrorized by him.

287
00:21:10,100 --> 00:21:14,500
He often used to say, I give ulcers. I don't get them.

288
00:21:15,980 --> 00:21:20,560
Farnsworth wasn't intimidated. He held his ground under increasing pressure.

289
00:21:21,200 --> 00:21:23,320
For once, Sarnoff had met his match.

290
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:28,460
Sarnoff gave in, and for several years, Farnsworth received royalties from RCA.

291
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:35,360
Sarnoff now had control of the patents that would help him reach his dream of a television in every home.

292
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:40,860
But events were about to show him just how daunting a challenge lay ahead.

293
00:21:47,860 --> 00:21:52,860
By the early 1930s, David Sarnoff was the king of the radio airwaves.

294
00:21:52,861 --> 00:21:55,860
RCA was selling millions of radios.

295
00:21:55,861 --> 00:22:00,860
They also owned the two biggest networks, NBC Blue and NBC Red.

296
00:22:00,861 --> 00:22:02,860
But Sarnoff had bigger plans.

297
00:22:02,861 --> 00:22:08,860
Sarnoff's vision for television simply was that he expected it to be in most American homes in the 1930s,

298
00:22:08,861 --> 00:22:11,860
not in the 1950s, because the technology was there.

299
00:22:11,861 --> 00:22:15,860
Sarnoff had Zworkin's system, and he was ready to promote it.

300
00:22:15,861 --> 00:22:18,860
The obstacles were greater than Sarnoff knew.

301
00:22:18,861 --> 00:22:25,860
TV signals could be sent by coaxial cable, but there was no reliable way to send the signal by broadcast.

302
00:22:25,861 --> 00:22:28,860
Besides, there was almost no public demand.

303
00:22:28,861 --> 00:22:31,860
Who needed pictures when you had radio?

304
00:22:32,860 --> 00:22:36,280
As long as baby must roam

305
00:22:36,281 --> 00:22:39,060
I promised him my way

306
00:22:39,061 --> 00:22:43,000
Radio fever was sweeping the nation, and Sarnoff faced a new threat,

307
00:22:43,260 --> 00:22:47,060
a man who would challenge him first in radio, then in television.

308
00:22:47,620 --> 00:22:49,800
The man was William S. Paley.

309
00:22:50,380 --> 00:22:52,740
Unlike Sarnoff, Paley was born into wealth.

310
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:57,400
The son of a cigar magnate, Paley had been given a gift in 1928,

311
00:22:57,401 --> 00:23:00,040
a fledgling radio network called CBS.

312
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:02,280
Great cars. Plymouth?

313
00:23:02,281 --> 00:23:04,240
Paley was a natural-born showman.

314
00:23:04,440 --> 00:23:06,760
He had little interest in the science of television,

315
00:23:07,160 --> 00:23:10,460
but he'd keep Sarnoff on his toes with his programming genius.

316
00:23:11,160 --> 00:23:12,980
The two men were fierce competitors

317
00:23:12,981 --> 00:23:15,600
and also had great respect for one another.

318
00:23:16,840 --> 00:23:20,180
This tension would help shape both radio and television,

319
00:23:20,181 --> 00:23:22,900
but for now, television was still hampered.

320
00:23:23,440 --> 00:23:24,740
Pictures were not sharp,

321
00:23:25,060 --> 00:23:27,520
and sending a consistent signal was still difficult.

322
00:23:28,420 --> 00:23:31,800
Engineers had to learn how to send the TV signal through trees

323
00:23:31,801 --> 00:23:34,040
and buildings and hills.

324
00:23:34,041 --> 00:23:36,240
Interference was everywhere.

325
00:23:36,241 --> 00:23:39,180
Even the electronic pulse from a car spark plug

326
00:23:39,181 --> 00:23:41,840
could interrupt the television signal.

327
00:23:41,841 --> 00:23:45,380
Scientists worked day and night trying to solve the problem.

328
00:23:45,381 --> 00:23:48,980
They made progress, but the price tag was alarming.

329
00:23:48,981 --> 00:23:54,960
Sarnoff had spent millions, and he was about to spend more.

330
00:23:54,961 --> 00:23:57,300
Other executives thought he was crazy,

331
00:23:57,301 --> 00:24:01,360
but Sarnoff was eager to begin broadcasting.

332
00:24:01,361 --> 00:24:06,580
On the third floor of Radio City, Studio 3H was converted for television broadcast.

333
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:11,780
Sarnoff, the perfectionist, was there to oversee all the details.

334
00:24:12,260 --> 00:24:21,020
On July 7, 1936, using the call letters W2XBS, a highly experimental broadcast was attempted.

335
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:27,180
Members of the press and ad agency executives sat in front of TVs set up in Radio City.

336
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:31,420
On screen, they saw dancers, singers, speeches,

337
00:24:31,680 --> 00:24:34,500
and films of army maneuvers to cover set changes.

338
00:24:35,460 --> 00:24:37,860
Performers had to wear green and purple makeup

339
00:24:37,861 --> 00:24:39,600
for the camera to capture their faces.

340
00:24:40,380 --> 00:24:42,580
Temperatures from the hot lights soared.

341
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:45,180
So at the end of this historic telecast,

342
00:24:46,420 --> 00:24:50,500
Sarnoff was soaking wet the lights were so hot.

343
00:24:50,580 --> 00:24:53,380
He had, in fact, changed his shirt three times

344
00:24:53,381 --> 00:24:54,540
during the telecast.

345
00:24:55,400 --> 00:25:00,180
The next day, the Herald Tribune sneered at the blurred and foggy images,

346
00:25:00,480 --> 00:25:03,480
but the Times found the demonstration extremely interesting.

347
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:07,800
Television still had technical and talent problems to overcome.

348
00:25:08,580 --> 00:25:11,720
To solve them, Sarnoff mobilized his team of researchers

349
00:25:11,721 --> 00:25:14,700
and turned the city of New York into a research laboratory.

350
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:18,440
Television would work. Sarnoff would make sure of that.

351
00:25:19,300 --> 00:25:21,240
Every kind of programming was tried.

352
00:25:21,241 --> 00:25:25,660
Here, a cameraman captures on film the very first television drama.

353
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:33,560
Sets were rickety, the acting was over-exaggerated,

354
00:25:33,920 --> 00:25:36,180
and the lights were so hot that bits of paper,

355
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:37,940
which were supposed to look like snow,

356
00:25:38,440 --> 00:25:40,720
stuck to the effusively sweating actors.

357
00:25:43,080 --> 00:25:46,760
In Radio City, the Rockettes were brought in for a broadcast,

358
00:25:47,220 --> 00:25:48,560
but had to be sent away.

359
00:25:48,561 --> 00:25:51,500
Only a handful would fit on the screen at one time.

360
00:25:52,440 --> 00:25:54,360
Finding talent wasn't easy.

361
00:25:54,780 --> 00:25:57,220
At first, television was a stigma for actors.

362
00:25:57,720 --> 00:26:02,160
Even an unknown out-of-work singer named Frank Sinatra turned down television offers.

363
00:26:02,560 --> 00:26:04,680
He didn't think there was much of a future in it.

364
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:10,200
And by the late 1930s, it looked like television might not catch on.

365
00:26:10,780 --> 00:26:12,180
Programming was intermittent.

366
00:26:12,620 --> 00:26:13,680
Sets were expensive.

367
00:26:14,020 --> 00:26:15,180
The signal was weak.

368
00:26:15,740 --> 00:26:17,180
Radio was still king.

369
00:26:17,181 --> 00:26:22,180
At CBS radio, Paley was now pulling ahead of Sarnoff and NBC.

370
00:26:22,181 --> 00:26:25,180
He had pulled in the best dramatists and newsmen to CBS

371
00:26:25,181 --> 00:26:30,180
with Edward R. Murrow and Orson Welles, and audiences responded.

372
00:26:30,181 --> 00:26:34,180
Sarnoff needed a public relations coup to take back the momentum.

373
00:26:34,181 --> 00:26:38,180
He soon found the perfect forum to sell his idea of television

374
00:26:38,181 --> 00:26:41,180
to a curious but skeptical public.

375
00:26:41,181 --> 00:26:46,180
26 million people visited the New York World's Fair of 1939.

376
00:26:46,181 --> 00:26:58,760
I hereby dedicate the World's Fair and I declare it open to all mankind.

377
00:27:00,140 --> 00:27:05,160
The 1939 World's Fair was a momentous occasion in television history.

378
00:27:05,980 --> 00:27:10,160
Most of the public had heard of television, few had actually seen it.

379
00:27:10,161 --> 00:27:17,380
The theme was the future, and the biggest corporations gathered to unveil the most sophisticated technological breakthroughs.

380
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:26,320
The world's first fluorescent bulb, nylon stockings, wonder bread, even a newfangled invention called plastic amazed the public.

381
00:27:27,060 --> 00:27:30,360
At RCA's pavilion, fairgoers could view television.

382
00:27:31,140 --> 00:27:33,580
For a small fortune, they could take one home.

383
00:27:34,180 --> 00:27:39,120
David Sarnoff took the occasion to announce the beginning of regular broadcasting in America.

384
00:27:39,121 --> 00:27:42,180
The historic moment was captured on audio tape.

385
00:27:42,920 --> 00:27:49,700
It is with a feeling of humbleness that I come to this moment of announcing the birth in this country

386
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:58,980
of a new art so important in its implications that it is bound to affect all society.

387
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:06,660
For two hours a week, the people of New York could now tune into regular broadcasts on NBC.

388
00:28:06,661 --> 00:28:08,660
There were no Nielsen ratings.

389
00:28:08,661 --> 00:28:12,660
No one knew who Lucille Ball or Jackie Gleason or Walter Cronkite were.

390
00:28:12,661 --> 00:28:18,660
There were only 200 sets in all of New York, but television was now a reality.

391
00:28:18,661 --> 00:28:19,361
Philo T.

392
00:28:19,362 --> 00:28:24,660
Farnsworth was mentioned at the World's Fair for his invention of the world's first incubator.

393
00:28:24,661 --> 00:28:29,660
The inventor who had done so much for television was all but forgotten.

394
00:28:29,661 --> 00:28:35,660
When Farnsworth, in retreat at his New England lake, heard that Sarnoff had taken credit for the birth of television,

395
00:28:35,661 --> 00:28:41,560
television, he remarked, the baby has been born with a beard.

396
00:28:41,561 --> 00:28:45,100
Progress in production methods for TV was slow.

397
00:28:45,101 --> 00:28:48,700
Even early attempts at commercials were fraught with production hazards.

398
00:28:48,701 --> 00:28:58,580
Ford, for example, tried to do an arty shot of a new 1940 Ford, or 41 Ford, behind a fish

399
00:28:58,581 --> 00:28:59,700
ball.

400
00:28:59,701 --> 00:29:03,440
And they forgot about how hot the studio lights would be.

401
00:29:03,441 --> 00:29:08,120
And so this very arty shot suddenly went on the air with a lot of dead fish at the bottom

402
00:29:08,121 --> 00:29:12,140
of a fishbowl, because they'd been killed by the water, which had actually been brought

403
00:29:12,141 --> 00:29:15,980
up to a boiling level by the studio lights.

404
00:29:15,981 --> 00:29:17,620
Your throat can tell.

405
00:29:17,621 --> 00:29:22,700
Despite the problems, broadcasting was emerging as a powerful and potent social force, and

406
00:29:22,701 --> 00:29:24,860
Washington was taking notice.

407
00:29:24,861 --> 00:29:29,640
The Federal Communications Commission had been formed to help shape radio and television.

408
00:29:29,641 --> 00:29:36,640
In 1940, the new FCC chairman, James Fly, wanted to keep a strong hand over the emerging world of television.

409
00:29:37,940 --> 00:29:42,020
Fly felt Sarnoff had become too influential, too monopolistic.

410
00:29:42,021 --> 00:29:48,640
He pushed an antitrust law through Congress to weaken Sarnoff's stranglehold on the broadcast industry.

411
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:52,300
Sarnoff wasn't prepared for this fight.

412
00:29:52,680 --> 00:29:55,640
He had cultivated scientists and entertained presidents.

413
00:29:56,340 --> 00:29:58,740
Congressmen were unfamiliar adversaries.

414
00:29:59,640 --> 00:30:04,380
The FCC's actions forced Sarnoff to sell one of RCA's two NBC networks.

415
00:30:05,220 --> 00:30:09,840
Lifesaver magnate Edwin Noble bought it and turned it into the fledgling ABC.

416
00:30:10,860 --> 00:30:13,820
The defeat taught Sarnoff a valuable lesson.

417
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:20,500
The next time the government came between him and his plans for a television in every home, he would be prepared.

418
00:30:20,501 --> 00:30:33,720
By the mid-1940s, the troops were coming home from war, and America was about to enter an era of prosperity and growth unparalleled in history.

419
00:30:34,960 --> 00:30:40,360
America was working harder and playing harder, and television would soon become a favorite pastime.

420
00:30:40,940 --> 00:30:43,520
By now, the technology was improving steadily.

421
00:30:43,521 --> 00:30:51,520
At RCA, Vladimir Zwarkin invented a more efficient camera tube that could capture an image with fewer hot lights.

422
00:30:51,521 --> 00:30:57,520
The signal was being improved, in part the result of progress made in battlefield communications.

423
00:30:57,521 --> 00:30:59,572
But progress had created problems.

424
00:30:59,573 --> 00:31:04,520
There were no picture quality standards, almost no programming, and very few sets.

425
00:31:04,521 --> 00:31:11,520
Limited to bars and department store windows, television tantalized, promising the wonders of technology.

426
00:31:11,521 --> 00:31:16,241
technology. And proud set owners explain television to admiring friends.

427
00:31:16,242 --> 00:31:17,880
There will probably be as

428
00:31:17,881 --> 00:31:22,023
many of these amateur explanations as there are set owners.

429
00:31:22,024 --> 00:31:24,480
But television was a wonderland few

430
00:31:24,481 --> 00:31:30,197
Americans could afford. RCA, under Sarnoff's guidance, was out ahead of the pack.

431
00:31:30,198 --> 00:31:30,898
But an

432
00:31:30,899 --> 00:31:34,025
upstart businessman from New Jersey named Alan B.

433
00:31:34,026 --> 00:31:37,360
Dumont was putting pressure on RCA. Dumont had a

434
00:31:37,361 --> 00:31:38,686
budding TV network.

435
00:31:38,687 --> 00:31:43,780
He also had a superior TV set with 600 lines of clear picture resolution.

436
00:31:44,460 --> 00:31:49,598
Sarnoff's old nemesis at the FCC, James Fly, hadn't yet decided on a standard.

437
00:31:49,599 --> 00:31:50,520
Would American

438
00:31:50,521 --> 00:31:54,038
TVs have RCA's 450 lines or Dumont's 600 lines?

439
00:31:54,039 --> 00:31:57,780
This critically important decision would determine

440
00:31:57,781 --> 00:32:03,360
how the signal would be sent and received, giving the owner of that particular system an edge.

441
00:32:03,980 --> 00:32:06,660
Sarnoff wanted his 450-line standard.

442
00:32:07,300 --> 00:32:09,180
Fly again tried to stop him,

443
00:32:09,181 --> 00:32:12,420
but this time the wily Sarnoff knew what to do.

444
00:32:13,060 --> 00:32:14,500
He cajoled Washington,

445
00:32:14,740 --> 00:32:17,300
now aware of the budding influence of TV,

446
00:32:17,720 --> 00:32:19,880
and eventually he got what he wanted.

447
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:23,660
Sarnoff's 450 lines were approved by the FCC.

448
00:32:24,980 --> 00:32:27,140
After two decades of development,

449
00:32:27,500 --> 00:32:30,000
Sarnoff's dream of a television in every home

450
00:32:30,001 --> 00:32:32,160
was still just that, a dream.

451
00:32:32,161 --> 00:32:34,960
And developing TV hadn't been cheap.

452
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:39,480
It was a black hole sucking in $200 million in research and development,

453
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:42,040
and there was very little to show in return.

454
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:45,060
Only $5 million in sets had been made.

455
00:32:45,760 --> 00:32:48,940
Sarnoff was so desperate that he would form an odd alliance

456
00:32:48,941 --> 00:32:51,600
with his rival broadcaster, Alan Dumont.

457
00:32:52,120 --> 00:32:57,020
The Dumont network had a superior field operation, and RCA had promotion.

458
00:32:57,021 --> 00:33:03,020
Together, Dumont and RCA arranged to broadcast the World Series of 1947.

459
00:33:03,021 --> 00:33:08,020
The historic series was captured by a young director, Harry Coyle,

460
00:33:08,021 --> 00:33:13,020
who experimented with multiple cameras, setting a precedent for sports coverage.

461
00:33:15,020 --> 00:33:20,020
The experimental broadcast worked beyond anyone's wildest imagination.

462
00:33:20,021 --> 00:33:25,020
All over New York, department stores and bars were filled with transfixed viewers.

463
00:33:25,021 --> 00:33:26,160
fixed viewers.

464
00:33:26,161 --> 00:33:29,400
Each game was more dramatic than the next.

465
00:33:29,401 --> 00:33:32,420
The bars and the appliance store windows

466
00:33:32,421 --> 00:33:36,040
are packed so thick that Paley, driving by in his limousine,

467
00:33:36,041 --> 00:33:38,300
had to ask the driver what that big crowd was.

468
00:33:38,301 --> 00:33:41,240
And when he was told there was people watching television,

469
00:33:41,241 --> 00:33:42,900
he actually asked the driver to stop,

470
00:33:42,901 --> 00:33:45,180
and he got out and walked over and stood

471
00:33:45,181 --> 00:33:52,180
in the back of the crowd to see what could keep people so fixed.

472
00:33:52,181 --> 00:33:55,200
With this historic first, television came of age.

473
00:33:55,680 --> 00:33:58,800
Suddenly, the doomsdayers began changing their minds.

474
00:33:59,140 --> 00:34:01,740
Maybe television could compete with radio.

475
00:34:02,540 --> 00:34:04,520
The demand for sets began to grow,

476
00:34:04,521 --> 00:34:07,760
but for most Americans, the cost was still too high.

477
00:34:08,140 --> 00:34:09,880
But that was about to change.

478
00:34:10,640 --> 00:34:13,560
Los Angeles car dealer Earl Madman Munz,

479
00:34:13,580 --> 00:34:17,160
who had earned his name with his wacky promotional stunts, stepped in.

480
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:20,640
The prospect for TV got his salesman's blood going.

481
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:24,980
In 1948, Muntz checked into New York's Warwick Hotel.

482
00:34:25,780 --> 00:34:29,340
He had a new set from each manufacturer delivered to his room.

483
00:34:29,341 --> 00:34:35,000
He tuned into a station and then systematically tested and interchanged parts.

484
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:38,740
Within one day, he had created a stripped-down set.

485
00:34:39,580 --> 00:34:44,360
Back in L.A., Muntz bought wholesale parts and a Muntz TV was born.

486
00:34:44,980 --> 00:34:49,500
The price? $170, hundreds less than his competitors.

487
00:34:49,501 --> 00:34:54,500
In no time, sales took off and others were forced to lower their prices.

488
00:34:54,501 --> 00:35:00,500
By the end of 1949, there were four million sets in America.

489
00:35:00,501 --> 00:35:05,500
So amazed were the new viewers, many were content to just stare at the test pattern.

490
00:35:05,501 --> 00:35:11,500
There were very few shows and those that were broadcast often didn't come in well.

491
00:35:11,501 --> 00:35:24,340
Now that many of the technical and legal challenges had been overcome, the search for talent was on.

492
00:35:25,260 --> 00:35:27,220
Executives didn't have to look very far.

493
00:35:27,480 --> 00:35:30,180
Vaudeville, a dying institution, was resuscitated.

494
00:35:30,660 --> 00:35:33,380
It was a talent pool America couldn't resist.

495
00:35:34,560 --> 00:35:40,840
Milton Berle, Bob Hope, Jerry Lewis, and Dean Martin were suddenly visitors in living rooms all across America.

496
00:35:40,841 --> 00:35:43,880
I want to say that it's a great, great thrill to get back on television.

497
00:35:44,540 --> 00:35:48,160
I've, uh, this is my third, I've had three shots on television.

498
00:35:48,700 --> 00:35:50,820
Fortunately, they all miss me, but I must say.

499
00:35:52,120 --> 00:35:54,180
Stars were being created overnight.

500
00:35:54,860 --> 00:35:56,660
Some made it, and some didn't.

501
00:35:56,680 --> 00:35:57,880
It's an easy way to find out.

502
00:35:58,080 --> 00:36:02,500
One popular producer, Mike Stokey, hired an aspiring actress named Norma Jean.

503
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:05,700
He had gotten her name from the UCLA Bulletin Board.

504
00:36:07,100 --> 00:36:09,760
So she worked very hard in rehearsal, memorized her lines,

505
00:36:09,761 --> 00:36:12,840
and on the air, she got stage fright and forgot her lines,

506
00:36:13,340 --> 00:36:14,960
and Stokey had to talk her through it,

507
00:36:15,020 --> 00:36:16,380
and finally at the end of the show,

508
00:36:16,460 --> 00:36:18,180
when the light on the camera went off,

509
00:36:18,240 --> 00:36:20,060
she collapsed into tears and said,

510
00:36:20,140 --> 00:36:22,200
Oh, Mr. Stokey, I'll never be an actress.

511
00:36:22,320 --> 00:36:23,280
I'm a complete failure.

512
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:27,340
A year later, Norma Jean was Marilyn Monroe,

513
00:36:27,780 --> 00:36:30,060
a star too big for the small screen.

514
00:36:31,980 --> 00:36:35,180
To attract viewers, the TV camera had to take them places

515
00:36:35,181 --> 00:36:36,380
they'd never been before.

516
00:36:36,640 --> 00:36:39,740
One draw was the first televised tour of the White House,

517
00:36:39,760 --> 00:36:43,660
House, personally conducted by President Truman.

518
00:36:43,661 --> 00:36:46,460
In the old days, it was really, it was really ghastly.

519
00:36:46,461 --> 00:36:49,260
Tons of equipment and light, heavy lights.

520
00:36:49,261 --> 00:36:52,460
On the White House tour with President Truman, which was the

521
00:36:52,461 --> 00:36:55,460
first time television was permitted in the White House,

522
00:36:55,461 --> 00:37:01,160
that was one of the problems we had to overcome in convincing

523
00:37:01,161 --> 00:37:04,860
the White House to let us in there at all.

524
00:37:04,861 --> 00:37:08,360
This demand for real-life events on television presented both

525
00:37:08,361 --> 00:37:10,860
an obstacle and an opportunity.

526
00:37:10,861 --> 00:37:14,680
If life was in color, why was TV limited to black and white?

527
00:37:14,681 --> 00:37:19,440
There were 11 million sets by 1950, all of them black and white.

528
00:37:19,441 --> 00:37:22,940
For Sarnoff, Paley, and Dumont, color was the new challenge.

529
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:33,340
The complex notion of breaking down the spectrum of color

530
00:37:33,341 --> 00:37:35,260
into manageable rays of electrons

531
00:37:35,261 --> 00:37:37,980
was a technological Mount Everest.

532
00:37:37,981 --> 00:37:42,140
Whoever managed to solve the problem first would wield tremendous power.

533
00:37:42,680 --> 00:37:47,280
CBS, RCA, and Dumont all put small armies of engineers to work on color.

534
00:37:47,960 --> 00:37:53,160
David Sarnoff had faced many battles, but none more fierce than the battle for color television.

535
00:37:54,080 --> 00:38:02,260
He often said, competition brings out the best in products and the worst in men.

536
00:38:02,261 --> 00:38:11,520
and he thought that the battle for color brought out really the toughest, meanest slugfest he'd ever encountered.

537
00:38:13,160 --> 00:38:16,480
Paley had gotten off to a head start in the battle for color.

538
00:38:17,140 --> 00:38:22,500
During World War II, he had spent money developing a simple, efficient, mechanical version of color.

539
00:38:23,200 --> 00:38:25,940
In lab tests, Paley's color version was impressive.

540
00:38:26,220 --> 00:38:30,340
So impressive, the FCC decided to give Paley the license for color.

541
00:38:31,120 --> 00:38:37,180
CBS was poised to sell millions of mechanical color television sets and beat Sarnoff to the punch.

542
00:38:37,640 --> 00:38:39,620
But Sarnoff put up a fight.

543
00:38:39,621 --> 00:38:47,780
He pointed out that Paley's mechanical color set was incompatible with the millions of electronic black and white sets already in use.

544
00:38:48,540 --> 00:38:53,500
He felt that new color programs should also be received on old black and white sets.

545
00:38:54,140 --> 00:38:57,780
After all, electronic television was the standard format.

546
00:38:57,781 --> 00:39:00,780
why go back to mechanical TV for color?

547
00:39:02,580 --> 00:39:04,780
Sarnoff believed his engineers could develop

548
00:39:04,781 --> 00:39:06,780
an electronic color television.

549
00:39:06,781 --> 00:39:09,720
The battle would go all the way to the Supreme Court,

550
00:39:09,721 --> 00:39:12,620
and again, Sarnoff would lose this fight.

551
00:39:12,621 --> 00:39:15,240
But he refused to give up.

552
00:39:15,241 --> 00:39:18,640
He gathered us all together, and he said,

553
00:39:18,641 --> 00:39:20,440
regardless of what the Supreme Court said,

554
00:39:20,441 --> 00:39:21,540
this system won't work.

555
00:39:21,541 --> 00:39:23,140
It can't be the television of the future.

556
00:39:23,141 --> 00:39:25,040
We're going to go ahead with the development

557
00:39:25,041 --> 00:39:27,780
of all electronic color television.

558
00:39:27,781 --> 00:39:35,780
Sarnoff organized his engineers and physicists into an operation with the feel and precision of a military operation.

559
00:39:35,781 --> 00:39:43,780
The fight for color would determine the future, not just of television, but computers, microsurgery, and even space exploration.

560
00:39:43,781 --> 00:39:46,780
A lot of money and prestige were at stake.

561
00:39:52,780 --> 00:39:56,780
By the early 1950s, the country was in the grip of a Cold War.

562
00:39:56,781 --> 00:40:01,400
hula hoops and drive-ins were sweeping the nation there were millions of

563
00:40:01,401 --> 00:40:05,960
television sets in American homes and David Sarnoff and William Paley were

564
00:40:05,961 --> 00:40:11,500
locked in fierce battle for control over each and every one color was the key

565
00:40:11,501 --> 00:40:16,420
Sarnoff was undeterred by his setbacks in developing color most thought it was

566
00:40:16,421 --> 00:40:22,200
impossible not Sarnoff at RCA he pushed his scientists driving them to invent

567
00:40:22,201 --> 00:40:27,320
electronic color television. Sarnoff was down here day after day standing

568
00:40:27,321 --> 00:40:31,140
standing under the white hot lights working with the scientists. A top

569
00:40:31,141 --> 00:40:37,080
American industrialist down in shirt sleeves sweating down there pushing the

570
00:40:37,081 --> 00:40:41,960
scientists urging them on get something off the breadboard for me. RCA finally

571
00:40:41,961 --> 00:40:46,260
did it they invented a whole new process inside the camera through an ingenious

572
00:40:46,261 --> 00:40:50,000
array of mirrors the image was broken down into the three primary colors and

573
00:40:50,001 --> 00:40:52,080
sent into three different tubes.

574
00:40:52,081 --> 00:40:53,620
When the electrons came back together,

575
00:40:53,621 --> 00:40:56,500
they would accurately reflect the true color image.

576
00:40:56,501 --> 00:40:59,320
The development of a tricolor picture tube took years.

577
00:40:59,321 --> 00:41:01,740
Even today, it's still considered one of the most amazing

578
00:41:01,741 --> 00:41:03,700
feats in industrial history.

579
00:41:03,701 --> 00:41:06,680
The FCC had no choice but to declare Sarnoff's version

580
00:41:06,681 --> 00:41:07,740
the new standard.

581
00:41:07,741 --> 00:41:10,280
Once again, the general had triumphed.

582
00:41:10,281 --> 00:41:13,780
Color TV was introduced on the market in 1954.

583
00:41:13,781 --> 00:41:15,880
But before color took off, television

584
00:41:15,881 --> 00:41:18,080
would begin its golden age of programming,

585
00:41:18,081 --> 00:41:20,320
and William Paley was blazing the trail.

586
00:41:20,321 --> 00:41:22,020
I, I, no talk.

587
00:41:22,021 --> 00:41:24,580
You must never talk when you're playing golf.

588
00:41:24,581 --> 00:41:25,480
Now don't cough either.

589
00:41:25,481 --> 00:41:26,880
Now look at me, look at me.

590
00:41:26,881 --> 00:41:27,720
Oh!

591
00:41:29,360 --> 00:41:30,600
Thanks in part to Paley,

592
00:41:30,601 --> 00:41:33,900
the tube was transforming the way America lived.

593
00:41:33,901 --> 00:41:37,260
There was a vividness to it and a mood to it.

594
00:41:37,261 --> 00:41:41,000
The suspense that television could offer

595
00:41:41,001 --> 00:41:44,840
by bringing it into your home in a raw, unpackaged state,

596
00:41:44,841 --> 00:41:46,880
with no one knowing what the outcome would be,

597
00:41:47,260 --> 00:41:50,940
made it almost addictive for many Americans.

598
00:41:51,440 --> 00:41:53,620
The impact was felt in every part of life.

599
00:41:53,740 --> 00:41:55,560
In New York, during the Jack Benny show,

600
00:41:55,720 --> 00:41:57,380
water usage dropped dramatically,

601
00:41:57,381 --> 00:41:59,680
then promptly surged when it was over.

602
00:42:00,160 --> 00:42:03,880
On Thursday nights, restaurants saw business drop off by 8 o'clock

603
00:42:03,881 --> 00:42:06,460
as people rushed home for Amos and Andy.

604
00:42:08,020 --> 00:42:11,560
Soon, audiences learned the new medium could be used to distort

605
00:42:11,561 --> 00:42:13,020
as well as to entertain.

606
00:42:13,021 --> 00:42:18,020
TV experienced growing pains, forcing the industry to change and grow up.

607
00:42:18,021 --> 00:42:25,020
On November 2, 1959, former quiz show contestant Charles Van Doren appeared before a House subcommittee.

608
00:42:25,021 --> 00:42:32,020
Winner of $129,000, Van Doren admitted that program officials had supplied him with questions and answers.

609
00:42:32,021 --> 00:42:37,020
Americans were learning not to trust the message, even if they loved the medium.

610
00:42:37,021 --> 00:42:37,920
loved the medium.

611
00:42:41,960 --> 00:42:44,260
Sputnik had shocked a confident America

612
00:42:44,261 --> 00:42:46,860
and launched a national race for space.

613
00:42:47,460 --> 00:42:48,660
Sarnoff, the Russian immigrant,

614
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:51,200
was determined to help America prove her mettle.

615
00:42:51,600 --> 00:42:53,400
As he had done so often before,

616
00:42:53,800 --> 00:42:55,180
Sarnoff led the charge,

617
00:42:55,440 --> 00:42:57,760
inspiring his scientists to find a way

618
00:42:57,761 --> 00:42:59,420
to send a signal through the atmosphere.

619
00:42:59,920 --> 00:43:01,240
After years of hard work,

620
00:43:01,320 --> 00:43:02,600
they were finally successful.

621
00:43:03,300 --> 00:43:05,920
Once in orbit, Telstar picks up a signal from Earth,

622
00:43:05,921 --> 00:43:09,860
magnifies it 10 billion times, and relays it to a receiving station.

623
00:43:10,140 --> 00:43:12,190
Pictures that were broadcast from the U.S.

624
00:43:12,191 --> 00:43:14,240
to Europe and back add remarkable clarity.

625
00:43:15,440 --> 00:43:21,040
The world was being brought together in ways undreamed of by television's original inventors.

626
00:43:21,460 --> 00:43:25,720
People shared triumph and tragedy together, all through this electronic box.

627
00:43:26,660 --> 00:43:34,340
It's a powerful medium when the chips are down emotionally or politically.

628
00:43:34,341 --> 00:43:40,240
The assassination of President Kennedy was very much of a case in point.

629
00:43:40,241 --> 00:43:44,860
An entire nation gathered around their television sets.

630
00:43:45,860 --> 00:43:48,380
And perhaps there was an even greater immediacy

631
00:43:48,381 --> 00:43:52,860
because the young president was so much better known thanks to television.

632
00:43:54,620 --> 00:43:57,020
Almost any event, no matter how remote,

633
00:43:57,140 --> 00:44:01,220
was now brought directly into the homes of millions in all its raw power.

634
00:44:01,221 --> 00:44:06,660
It's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

635
00:44:07,560 --> 00:44:10,500
I shall resign the presidency effective at noon.

636
00:44:10,501 --> 00:44:13,860
As viewers absorbed worldwide events in the intimacy of their homes,

637
00:44:14,040 --> 00:44:18,040
few had ever heard of the men who made this stunning act of magic possible.

638
00:44:18,900 --> 00:44:22,120
The boy genius Philo T. Farnsworth was all but forgotten.

639
00:44:22,680 --> 00:44:25,460
When he appeared on Gary Moore's I've Got a Secret,

640
00:44:25,860 --> 00:44:28,000
the contestant couldn't even identify him.

641
00:44:28,001 --> 00:44:32,300
Philo had to tell him, I was the man who invented television.

642
00:44:33,060 --> 00:44:39,000
The genius farm boy died in 1970 at the age of 64, depressed and in ill health.

643
00:44:39,360 --> 00:44:44,460
The last 10 years of his life, it was said he didn't want the word television uttered in his home.

644
00:44:45,620 --> 00:44:52,660
David Sarnoff, his place in history secure, could sit back and look at how his passion for television had changed the world.

645
00:44:53,220 --> 00:44:55,180
The boy who began with the Morse code key

646
00:44:55,181 --> 00:44:58,080
late in his life helped arrange telecasts from the moon.

647
00:44:59,860 --> 00:45:03,520
Late in his life, Sarnoff was still able to peer far into the future.

648
00:45:03,880 --> 00:45:09,200
As early as 1949, he envisioned the world of wireless phone, fax, and image.

649
00:45:09,201 --> 00:45:13,340
Pocket-sized radio instruments will enable individuals

650
00:45:13,341 --> 00:45:16,300
to communicate with anyone, anywhere.

651
00:45:17,540 --> 00:45:21,980
All through the 1950s and 60s, Sarnoff kept up his unique partnership

652
00:45:21,981 --> 00:45:23,160
with Vladimir Zwarkin.

653
00:45:23,580 --> 00:45:25,380
The young scientist from St. Petersburg

654
00:45:25,381 --> 00:45:27,240
had once beamed electron particles

655
00:45:27,241 --> 00:45:29,520
as horse and buggies passed by his window.

656
00:45:30,120 --> 00:45:31,700
Now he was pushing the edges

657
00:45:31,701 --> 00:45:33,680
of interstellar microcircuitry.

658
00:45:34,260 --> 00:45:36,500
The two men often looked back with nostalgia

659
00:45:36,501 --> 00:45:39,440
on their fortuitous first meeting in 1923.

660
00:45:40,500 --> 00:45:41,800
You were a good salesman,

661
00:45:42,800 --> 00:45:44,320
and I was a good dreamer.

662
00:45:45,260 --> 00:45:47,120
What began as fantasies,

663
00:45:47,240 --> 00:45:50,120
Sarnoff, Zwarkin, Farnsworth, and countless others

664
00:45:50,121 --> 00:45:51,600
turned into a reality

665
00:45:51,601 --> 00:45:53,800
that continues to shape our lives.

666
00:45:54,360 --> 00:45:57,400
No one can deny the impact it has had on the world.

667
00:45:58,540 --> 00:46:02,140
Television has totally homogenized our culture.

668
00:46:02,580 --> 00:46:03,940
The corollary of that, however,

669
00:46:04,060 --> 00:46:06,280
is that it has brought people closer together

670
00:46:06,281 --> 00:46:08,460
so that we understand each other

671
00:46:08,461 --> 00:46:10,280
much better than we ever did before.

672
00:46:10,840 --> 00:46:13,260
What once seemed like an unimaginable miracle

673
00:46:13,261 --> 00:46:15,360
is now part of everyday life.

674
00:46:15,460 --> 00:46:18,820
This small box has made a huge impact on life

675
00:46:18,821 --> 00:46:20,260
in every corner of the globe.

676
00:46:20,261 --> 00:46:23,460
Our world has grown smaller because of television,

677
00:46:23,461 --> 00:46:27,100
and our view of it has grown immeasurably larger.

678
00:46:50,260 --> 00:46:57,240
I don't know.

